 - **Roles.** Each role names the stage: `model`, `cli`, `runtime`, `rank`, `max_tokens`, `effort` and `fallbacks` (CLI names, in order). A fallback names the model: `[fallback.<cli>]` has `model`, `runtime`, and for `agent` `max_calls` and `max_minutes`. A fallback try uses the role's rank, cap and effort. `rank`, `max_tokens` and `effort` on a fallback are refused when the file is loaded, and so are `order` and a `[<skill>.<cli>]` table. A role's `pool` lists `[member.<name>]` models (cli, model, runtime), tried after the role's own model and before its fallbacks, so several models may run on one CLI. A role with `rotate = true` is ordered by the picker (`reviewpool`): tier 1 differs in model line (`[families]`, a prefix table) from the author and the previous round, tier 2 from the author only, tier 3 is the author's line, the flagged fallback, its exact model last; no try is dropped for the author and the chain never stops on it. `author` only names the role whose first choice stands in for the writer when no row records it. A role, fallback or member that names an unknown model or CLI fails when the file is loaded. The roles are `idea`, `idea-challenge`, `design`, `design-challenge`, `raft`, `implementation`, `code-review`, `stream-review` and `final-review`. `./zn idea` is the idea stage: its calls go through the same supervised model step as `./zn dispatch`.
 - **The agent credential** (the user's "Bot token, no bypass"). A model step runs with the agent's own GitHub token (a fine-grained token or a GitHub App's, read from `$ZN_AGENT_TOKEN_FILE`, else `~/.config/zn/agent-token`, else the keychain item `zn-dispatch-agent`); without one no CLI starts. It can push branches and open pull requests and can't merge, bypass `main`'s protection or write protection or rulesets. The step's `GH_TOKEN` and `GITHUB_TOKEN` are that token, gh reads an empty configuration folder of the step's own, and git's credential helpers answer with that token only. Not enforceable: the agent runs as the user, so a process that goes looking can still read the user's own credentials (the keychain, `~/.config/gh`); only a separate OS user would stop that, and the tripwire during a run and the server's protection of `main` are what catch its use.
 - **Tracker state** has one definition (`z-nile/engine/trackerstate.luau`), which the wrapper, the broker, the tripwire and the merge gates all use: git's raw diff with renames off (`diff-tree -r --raw --no-renames`), every path under a project root's folder (with the slash) or exactly `z-nile/next`, plain (ASCII, no empty, `.`, `..` or `.git` component), only regular files (no symlink, no submodule), no `.gitattributes`, `.gitmodules` or `.models.toml` in any case, no added path that differs only in case from another; and each item's sequestered files (its `check.sh` and `acceptance/`, in that exact case) judged per item: the same set, each unchanged or, when the item's folder moved, as `paths.rewrite` writes it for that move; only a new item adds them.
